# tools/ales_reverb_shelf_witness.rish — the shelf: a two-band tone shelf on the reverberant tail, the reverb's EQ (ALES204).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES204.
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-112557_fill-ales204-lotus-reverb-shelf.md
# Stands on: lotus/reverb_preset.rye (ALES190 — the named-room wet, run over the span unchanged); lotus/shelf.rye (ALES41 — the two-band tone shelf,
# its precheck and its BadCoeff / BadGain / BadRange, the shelving applied to the wet span); lotus/reverb_tone.rye (ALES198 — the plain high-cut
# darkness knob this rung generalizes into a full shelving EQ); lotus/clock.rye (ALES5 — the sample clock, converting the span); lotus/timeline.rye
# (ALES2 — the Clip, its max_clip bounding the span and its saturate the shelf's single rail).
#
# lotus/reverb_shelf.rye gives the reverberant tail a proper SHELVING EQ — two knobs, bass and treble, each boosting OR cutting its own band of the
# tail independently, so the wash sits warm and dark, bright and airy, or thinned of mud. ALES198's darkness knob was a plain first-order high-cut,
# one direction only (the tail could be darkened, never brightened, and the low wash never lifted); this rung is the two-band shelf every real reverb
# carries. It reverberates the span wet in place through ALES190's reverb_preset UNCHANGED, then tone-shelves the wet with ALES41's proven two-band
# shelf: bass == unity AND treble == unity is the identity, a bass boost lifts the low wash, a treble cut darkens the tail. It invents no new audio
# arithmetic — every shelved sample is written by a proven stage, each band scaled in the wide i64 domain and saturated ONCE (ALES8's fader lesson) —
# and it prechecks the shelf setting (the SAME guards ALES41 raises) BEFORE the wet is written, so a bad setting leaves the clip DRY (atomic). The
# selftest proves: at bass == unity AND treble == unity the span equals reverb_preset byte for byte; for a real shelf reverb_shelf equals reverb_preset
# then shelf.shelf byte for byte and the shelved tail differs from the un-shelved wet; a treble-cut tail's high-frequency roughness is strictly less
# than the wet's (the treble cut darkens); a bass-boost tail's total energy is strictly greater than the wet's (the bass boost lifts); silence stays
# silence; and the faults forward atomic (an illegal coefficient BadCoeff, a zero band denominator BadGain with the clip still dry, a span past the
# clip DurationTooLong, an out-of-range span BadRange). Purely local — one clip, a validated clock; no network, no keys, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_reverb_shelf_witness.rish

say "ales-reverb-shelf: Language EN — ALES198 gave the reverb its DARKNESS knob, a plain first-order high-cut that could only darken the tail, never brighten it, and could never reach the low wash to lift or trim it. Yet the tone control a keeper actually turns on a reverb is a proper SHELVING EQ — two knobs, bass and treble, each boosting OR cutting its own band independently. This rung is that shelf — and it invents no new primitive, since ALES41's two-band shelf (the low/high split of ALES40 scaled per band and clamped once) already stands, so the reverb EQ is a clean composition of two proven stages."
say "ales-reverb-shelf: Style Radiant — the identity edge is stated positively and proven exact: at bass == unity AND treble == unity the span equals reverb_preset byte for byte (a flat EQ is the un-shelved reverb); a bass boost lifts the low wash so the shelved tail's total energy strictly exceeds the wet's, and a treble cut darkens the tail so its high-frequency roughness falls strictly below the wet's — the very direction ALES198's high-cut shared, now joined by the boost and the low band the high-cut could never reach. The shelf gives the room two colours where the darkness knob gave one."
say "ales-reverb-shelf: Lens TAME — one clip of i16 PCM, a validated clock, and ALES190's fixed banks; NO new audio arithmetic — the shelf is ALES41's proven two-band shelf over the proven wet, each band scaled in the wide i64 domain and saturated ONCE; ReverbShelfError = reverb_preset.ReverbPresetError || shelf.ShelfError, the shelf setting PRECHECKED before the wet is written (the same guards ALES41 raises) so a bad shelf leaves the clip dry (atomic), DurationTooLong / BadRange / BadCoeff / BadGain by name before any write; siloed to lotus/, no network, no keys, no funds, no device. A wet-tail shelf; the split is a fraction per sample index (a clock-timed crossover in hertz is a later rung, as reverb_ms followed the index-named reverb)."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/reverb_shelf"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/reverb_shelf.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-reverb-shelf: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/reverb_shelf.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-reverb-shelf: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-reverb-shelf: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-reverb-shelf' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-reverb-shelf: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-reverb-shelf line"

# This rung runs ALES190's reverb_preset over its public API for the wet — re-prove the named-room preset still stands green beside it.
let preset = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/reverb_preset.rye -femit-bin=lotus/bin/reverb_preset && lotus/bin/reverb_preset selftest 2>&1 | grep -q 'GREEN ales-reverb-preset' && echo yes"]
assert (trim preset.out) == "yes" else "ales-reverb-shelf: the ALES190 named-room preset regressed under its shelf"

# And re-prove ALES41's two-band tone shelf still stands green — the shelving it applies to the wet (its precheck now shared with this rung).
let shelf = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/shelf.rye -femit-bin=lotus/bin/shelf && lotus/bin/shelf selftest 2>&1 | grep -q 'GREEN ales-shelf' && echo yes"]
assert (trim shelf.out) == "yes" else "ales-reverb-shelf: the ALES41 two-band shelf regressed under its reverb shelf"

# And re-prove ALES198's plain darkness knob still stands green — the one-direction high-cut this rung generalizes into a shelf.
let tone = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/reverb_tone.rye -femit-bin=lotus/bin/reverb_tone && lotus/bin/reverb_tone selftest 2>&1 | grep -q 'GREEN ales-reverb-tone' && echo yes"]
assert (trim tone.out) == "yes" else "ales-reverb-shelf: the ALES198 darkness knob regressed beside its shelf"

say "ales-reverb-shelf: GREEN — the shelf: a two-band tone shelf on the reverberant tail, the reverb's EQ. Reverberate the span wet in place through ALES190's reverb_preset unchanged, then tone-shelf the wet with ALES41's proven two-band shelf — bass == unity AND treble == unity the identity, a bass boost lifting the low wash, a treble cut darkening the tail — no new audio arithmetic, every shelved sample written by a proven stage. Proven: at bass == unity AND treble == unity exactly reverb_preset byte for byte; for a real shelf reverb_shelf equals reverb_preset then shelf.shelf byte for byte and the shelved tail differs from the un-shelved wet; a treble-cut tail's roughness is strictly less than the wet's (darkens); a bass-boost tail's energy is strictly greater than the wet's (lifts); silence stays silence; an illegal coefficient refuses BadCoeff, a zero band denominator BadGain with the clip still dry (prechecked before the wet is written), a span past the clip DurationTooLong, an out-of-range span BadRange, each leaving the clip untouched; the ALES190 preset, the ALES41 shelf, and the ALES198 darkness knob stay GREEN beside it. A stereo tone shelf (the same two-band shelf over both channels, one shared bass, treble, and split, the image held) is the thin twin next."
